# 📋 QA 360° Engineering Guidelines - Cursor Rules Configuration

## Professional Identity Context
# As: Senior Software Engineer (IFSP São Carlos) & QA Analyst at Foursys - Equifax Boa Vista Project
# Specialty: Software Quality with focus on Automation (QA), Processes and RPA
# Primary Stack: Robot Framework (Python), Selenium, Cypress, Playwright, Node.js, CI/CD (GitHub Actions)

## Core Philosophy - QA 360° Strategy
# Beyond Bug Detection: QA role is strategic involving risk management, business analysis and technical leadership
# Shift Left Testing: Validation starts at requirements (User Stories) and technical design phases
# Risk Management & Technical Debt: Maintain full project visibility. Items like "0% test coverage" are mapped as technical debt, prioritized by risk with estimated correction effort in roadmaps

## Automation Standards and Resilience Patterns
# Smart Waits Implementation:
# - Never use hardcoded sleep() functions
# - Implement dynamic waits based on UI state
# - Use explicit waits for element presence, visibility, and interactability
# - Apply polling intervals appropriate to the application response time

# Retry Logic Requirements:
# - Implement retry mechanisms for critical flows with intermittent failures
# - Configure exponential backoff strategies
# - Set maximum retry attempts (typically 3-5)
# - Log retry attempts for debugging purposes

# Fallback Strategies:
# - For authentication/login failures, implement alternative modes (anonymous mode)
# - Design graceful degradation paths
# - Ensure process continuity even when primary flows fail
# - Document fallback triggers and recovery procedures

# Dependency Management:
# - Pin driver versions (ChromeDriver, GeckoDriver) to specific releases
# - Use version locking in package managers (package-lock.json, requirements.txt)
# - Regular dependency audit for compatibility issues
# - Maintain controlled execution environments

## Documentation and Code Standards
# Living Documentation (README.md):
# - Include quality metrics and coverage percentages
# - Add security status (CVE scan results)
# - Document sprint roadmaps and improvement plans
# - Keep documentation updated with each release

# Definition of Done (DoD):
# - TypeScript type validation must pass
# - Linting rules enforced (ESLint/TSLint)
# - Clean build with zero errors
# - Functional tests execution successful
# - Security scan completed with no critical vulnerabilities
# - Performance benchmarks met

# Performance Optimization:
# - Monitor bundle size continuously
# - Implement Code Splitting for large applications
# - Apply Lazy Loading for non-critical components
# - Optimize loading times and resource consumption
# - Use performance monitoring tools (Lighthouse, Web Vitals)

## Security and Compliance (LGPD Focus)
# Security Priority:
# - Treat identified vulnerabilities as "Critical Priority"
# - Resolve security issues before new core features
# - Regular CVE scanning and dependency updates
# - Security testing integrated in CI/CD pipeline

# Privacy Compliance:
# - All development must consider LGPD regulations
# - Special attention to third-party data projects (credit bureaus)
# - Data minimization principles applied
# - Privacy impact assessments for new features
# - Secure data handling and encryption standards

## Code Quality Enforcement Rules

### TypeScript Standards
# - Strict type checking enabled (strict: true)
# - No implicit any types allowed
# - Explicit return types for all functions
# - Interface-first approach for complex objects
# - Generic types used appropriately

### Testing Requirements
# - Minimum 80% code coverage for new features
# - Unit tests for all business logic
# - Integration tests for API interactions
# - E2E tests for critical user flows
# - Test data isolation and cleanup

### Error Handling
# - Comprehensive error logging with context
# - User-friendly error messages
# - Graceful degradation strategies
# - Monitoring and alerting for production errors

### Performance Benchmarks
# - Page load time < 3 seconds
# - Bundle size < 2MB for main chunk
# - First Contentful Paint < 1.8 seconds
# - Cumulative Layout Shift < 0.1

## CI/CD Pipeline Requirements
# - Automated testing on every pull request
# - Security scanning integrated
# - Performance regression detection
# - Deployment rollback capability
# - Environment parity between stages

## Technical Debt Management
# - Track technical debt items with JIRA/GitHub issues
# - Assign risk scores (High/Medium/Low)
# - Estimate effort in story points
# - Include in sprint planning discussions
# - Regular debt reduction initiatives

## Review and Compliance Checklist
# Before merging any code:
# ☐ Security scan passed
# ☐ Performance benchmarks met
# ☐ Test coverage requirements satisfied
# ☐ Documentation updated
# ☐ LGPD compliance verified
# ☐ Technical debt assessment completed
# ☐ Peer code review conducted
# ☐ Definition of Done criteria fulfilled

## Emergency Procedures
# Critical Bug Response:
# - Immediate rollback capability
# - Incident response team notification
# - Root cause analysis within 24 hours
# - Post-mortem documentation required

# Security Incident Protocol:
# - Immediate isolation of affected systems
# - Stakeholder notification within 2 hours
# - Forensic analysis and remediation
# - Regulatory reporting if required

## Continuous Improvement
# Regular retrospectives on:
# - Test effectiveness and coverage gaps
# - Automation reliability and maintenance costs
# - Process bottlenecks and improvement opportunities
# - Security posture and emerging threats
# - Team skills development and knowledge sharing

# Apply these rules consistently across all development activities
# Quality is built-in, not added at the end
# Every line of code contributes to the overall system reliability